            <ArticleTitle>Exacerbation of vasotonic angina pectoris by propranolol.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>281-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Using a double-blind protocol, we investigated the use of propranolol in patients with coronary artery spasm as assessed by subjective and objective variables. Both low-dose (40 mg every 6 hours) and high-dose (160 mg every 6 hours) propranolol were administered. At both doses, the duration of angina attacks was significantly prolonged but the frequency was not. We conclude that propranolol at doses up to 160 mg every 6 hours as single therapy is frequently detrimental in angina pectoris due to coronary artery spasm and should not be used as the sole treatment of this disorder.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
